 ### Ray Unification
-Ray Unification is a technique to map every ray starting location onto a sphere circumscribed around the view cell, ensuring that identical rays have identical depth values regardless of the camera position, easing the task for the oracle network [S3]. For arbitrary views looking outside the view cell, the ray origin is mapped to the circumscribed sphere. For 360-degree object captures, the scheme is applied inversely by placing the circumscribed sphere around the object [S3]. Without ray unification, the same ray is encoded differently depending on the starting location, requiring different depth values [S3]. Ray unification adds about 0.6 dB in PSNR compared to no unification [S3].
+Ray Unification is a technique to map every ray starting location onto a sphere circumscribed around the view cell, ensuring that identical rays have identical depth values regardless of the camera position, easing the task for the oracle network [S3]. For arbitrary views looking outside the view cell, the ray origin is mapped to the circumscribed sphere [S3]. For 360-degree object captures, the scheme is applied inversely by placing the circumscribed sphere around the object [S3]. Without ray unification, the same ray is encoded differently depending on the starting location, requiring different depth values [S3]. Ray unification adds about 0.6 dB in PSNR compared to no unification [S3].
 
